.TH velcheck "" "Command"
.SH NAME
velcheck \- prove a vellum drawing set, or a stencil library
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBvelcheck\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR
.br
\fBvelcheck \-sym\fR \fIlib.sym ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
velcheck -- prove a vellum drawing set, or a stencil library
Usage:	velcheck file.d ...	judge the drawings
	velcheck -sym lib.sym ...	judge the stencils
	One finding per line on stdout; the EXIT STATUS is the
	finding count, so make(1) gates on it like cc -Werror.
.HE
.B velcheck
proves a set of drawings made with
.B vellum
before any paper moves.
It writes one finding per line on standard output as
.IR file ": " message ,
and its EXIT STATUS is the finding count (capped at 254), so
.BR make (1)
gates on it exactly the way it gates on
.BR cc " " \-Werror :
.DS
.ta 8n
	check: ; velcheck amp?.d
	print: check ; velplot amp?.d | lpr
.DE
A clean run says so on standard error and exits 0.
The file name
.B \-
is standard input.
.SH "DRAWING FINDINGS"
.DS
.ta 4n
	duplicate designators \- across the whole SET, naming the
	other sheet
	a designated part with no value
	dangling wire ends: an endpoint touching no pin, no other
	wire and no net-name marker
	symbol codes no loaded library defines \- the lines the
	loader would silently DROP, caught by re-reading the file
	sheets whose \fBU\fR units disagree with the set's
	single-pin nets, and unconnected pins
.DE
.PP
Then the ERC, between TYPED pins (see
.BR symedit ):
two outputs on one net conflict, an output must not drive a power
rail, and a net whose typed pins are all inputs is undriven.
The rules fire only where the library types its pins, so an old
library produces silence, not noise, and a half-typed one checks
exactly as far as it is typed.
.PP
The CONDUCTOR findings \- dangling ends, nets, pins, ERC \- apply only
to a sheet that contains WIRES.
A diagram sheet (flowchart, floor plan, structure chart) uses stencils
whose pins are attachment points, not terminals, and flagging those
would be noise rather than findings.
.SH "LIBRARY FINDINGS"
.B \-sym
proves the stencil LIBRARIES the same way, and it matters more: a
wrong stencil is wrong in EVERY drawing.
It reads only the libraries it is given \- the standard set is not
loaded underneath \- so ``defined in both'' means these files.
.DS
.ta 4n
	a code defined twice, or defined in two of the libraries given
	a symbol with no geometry
	a designator prefix with no pins
	a pin off a whole grid unit, or a pin name that repeats
	a pin left untyped in a library that types the others
	pins past the eight the loader keeps
	symbols the loader DROPPED because a pool filled
.DE
The last two nothing else can say, and the loader has always done both
silently \- after which the load ORDER decides which stencil a drawing
gets.
.SH EXAMPLES
.DS
.ta 8n
	velcheck amp1.d amp2.d
	velcheck -sym pid.sym
	velcheck -sym /usr/vellum/sym/*.sym
.DE
The second form is the gate in /usr/vellum/etc/library.mk, so a wrong
stencil never reaches a drawing.
The third finds the cross-library duplicate (``code R defined in both
discrete.sym and power.sym'') that no single file can produce.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velnet ,
.B veldiff ,
.B velsym ,
.B symedit ,
.B make
.SH NOTES
.B symedit
has the same findings behind its
.B Check
key, for the library open on the board.
.PP
A drawing that fails to load at all is an error, not a finding: the
program says so and exits 1.
